= Jatamansi

_Scope: This entry treats_ Nardostachys jatamansi _(D. Don) DC. (= N. grandiflora DC.), family Caprifoliaceae (formerly Valerianaceae) — the_ spikenard / Indian nard / Himalayan nard / Indian spikenard _of the classical world. The drug is the_ dried rhizome _and root system, prized for its dense aromatic-musk-and-resin scent. This is a substance of unusual cross-cultural significance: classical in_ all four canonical traditions _and substantially the same drug in each — Ayurvedic_ jaṭāmāṃsī _(Charaka), Tibb_ sunbul al-ṭīb _(Avicenna), Greco-Roman_ nardus indica / spica nardi _(Dioscorides, Pliny, biblical "spikenard" of Mark 14:3 and John 12:3), Chinese 甘松 gānsōng (Bencao Gangmu) — making it one of the cleanest cross-system convergences in the materia medica. Native to the high Himalayan alpine zone (3000–5000 m); reached the Roman world and the Han Chinese world via long-distance Silk-Road trade as a luxury aromatic. Modern conservation: CITES Appendix II listed; wild populations are endangered from over-collection — flagged for ethical sourcing._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Nardostachys jatamansi_ (D. Don) DC. (= _Nardostachys grandiflora_ DC.; older _Valeriana jatamansi_ Jones, _Patrinia jatamansi_); family Caprifoliaceae (modern APG) — formerly Valerianaceae (the herb is part of the modern valerian sister-clade, accounting for the closely-paralleled sedative-nervine register). The drug is the *dried rhizome and rootlets* (_Nardostachyos rhizoma_), with the matted, hair-like rootlets covering the rhizome surface — the basis of the Sanskrit name _jaṭāmāṃsī_ ("matted-hair flesh"). The genus name *Nardostachys* compounds Greek νάρδος (_nárdos_, "nard, spikenard") + στάχυς (_stáchys_, "spike, ear of grain"), literally "spike of nard" — recapitulating the classical Latin _spica nardi_. Latin _nardus_ from Greek νάρδος, itself most likely a loan ultimately from a Northwest Semitic source (Hebrew נֵרְדְּ _nēred_; Akkadian _lardu_), with Sanskrit *नलद (nalada)* either a parallel Indo-Iranian inheritance or a back-loan from Semitic via Iranian — the etymological direction is unsettled, but the *Mediterranean-to-Mesopotamia-to-India* trade-route circulation of the *word and the substance* is clear and well-attested.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short (s.v. _nardus_); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (s.v. νάρδος); Mayrhofer (s.v. _nalada-_). The Semitic-Greek-Latin-Sanskrit etymological circulation is one of the canonical Silk-Road / Persian-Gulf trade etymologies — the substance and the word travel together. The Caprifoliaceae / Valerianaceae taxonomic shift follows the modern APG classification.]

*English:* _Spikenard_ (the canonical biblical-translation term, via _spica nardi_); _Indian spikenard_, _Indian nard_, _muskroot_; in modern Western herbal: _jatamansi_ (the Sanskrit term increasingly preserved as a calque, especially in Ayurvedic-adjacent English-language pharmacy). The Wycliffe and King James biblical "spikenard" translates Greek νάρδου πιστικῆς (_nárdou pistikēs_, "true nard") of Mark 14:3 and John 12:3 — referring most likely to the high-grade _Nardostachys jatamansi_ rhizome oil imported from the Himalayan range. The Old English _narde_ enters via Latin / Greek.#footnote[_OED_ (s.v. "spikenard, n.", "nard, n."). The biblical translation tradition consistently renders νάρδος as "spikenard"; the species identification with _N. jatamansi_ is the modern philological consensus, though alternate identifications (_N. grandiflora_ as a distinct species; or in some early modern sources, lavender / spike-lavender, _Lavandula stoechas_, a confusing partial overlap in medieval European usage) are noted.]

*Sanskrit:* the herb has *many* Sanskrit names — reflecting its prominence in classical Indian medicine and culture.
- *जटामांसी (jaṭāmāṃsī)* — "matted-hair flesh," for the rhizome's appearance, covered with dense brown root-fibres resembling matted ascetic hair. The canonical and most-used name in classical and modern Ayurvedic literature.
- *नलदा (naladā)* — "the nard-giving" (Vedic name, possibly preserving the Indo-European root shared with Greek νάρδος; one of the oldest attested Sanskrit names for the herb).
- *माँसी (māṃsī)* — "fleshy" (short form of jaṭāmāṃsī).
- *तपस्विनी (tapasvinī)* — "the ascetic" / "the practitioner of tapas" — for the herb's use by yogis in meditation regimens; the name preserves the herb's classical association with concentration, sleep, and spirit-calming.
- *भूतजटा (bhūtajaṭā)* — "earth-matted-hair."
- *मेधा (medhā)* — sometimes used; aligns with the herb's _medhya_ (intellect-promoting) action.
- *पिशाची (piśācī)*, *चक्रकोष्ठा (cakrakoṣṭhā)* — additional classical names in some nighaṇṭus.#footnote[Monier-Williams (s.v. _jaṭāmāṃsī_, _naladā_, _māṃsī_, _tapasvinī_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Karpūrādi-varga_ (the cooling-aromatic group); Charaka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 4 and 27; the multiple Sanskrit names reflect the herb's prominence and the classical _paryāya_ (synonym) tradition of giving aromatic herbs many descriptive names.]

*Chinese:* *甘松 (gānsōng)*, "sweet pine" — the canonical Chinese drug name. The Chinese 甘松 is conventionally identified with *Nardostachys chinensis* Batal. (sometimes treated as a subspecies / variety of _N. jatamansi_), a closely related Chinese species; the modern Chinese pharmacopoeia treats _N. jatamansi_ and _N. chinensis_ as both qualifying for 甘松 use, with substantial pharmacological overlap. The drug is the dried rhizome, with broadly the same profile as the Indian / Tibb / Greco-Roman drug. 甘松 appears in the _Tang Bencao_ (唐本草, 659) and is treated extensively in the _Bencao Gangmu_; pre-Tang appearance is debated but the herb is essentially a Tang-era introduction or systematization in Chinese pharmacy, reflecting Tang-dynasty Silk-Road trade in Himalayan aromatics.#footnote[甘松 in _Tang Bencao_ (新修本草, 唐本草, 659) — possibly the first Chinese pharmacopoeial appearance; _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The _N. jatamansi_ / _N. chinensis_ identification: modern Chinese pharmacopoeia treats both as qualifying. The Tang-Silk-Road import history aligns with the broader pattern of Himalayan aromatic introductions to Chinese pharmacy in the 7th–9th c.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb register):* *سُنبُل الطِّيب (sunbul al-ṭīb)*, "the fragrant spike" — the canonical Tibb name, from Avicenna onwards. Also *سُنبُل هِندي (sunbul hindī)*, "Indian spike," explicitly marking the herb's Himalayan origin. Persian: سُنبُل الطیب (_sonbol al-ṭīb_), سُنبُل هِندی (_sonbol-e hendī_). Urdu / Hakeemi: سُنبُل الطیب (_sunbul al-ṭīb_); جٹاماسی (_jaṭāmāsī_, a borrowing from Sanskrit / Hindi). The Arabic _sunbul_ literally means "spike" (the same image as Greek στάχυς, Latin _spica_) — *all four classical traditions name the herb identically as the spike-of-nard*, one of the cleanest pan-classical naming convergences in the materia medica.#footnote[Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II, _sunbul_ entries; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, _sunbul_ and _sunbul hindī_ entries. The naming convergence (νάρδου στάχυς ↔ _spica nardi_ ↔ _sunbul al-ṭīb_ ↔ _jaṭāmāṃsī_ "matted-fleshy-spike") is striking: four traditions independently or interchangeably name the same Himalayan rhizome as the "fragrant spike."]

*Tibetan:* *སྤང་སྤོས་ (spang spos)*, "meadow incense" — the Tibetan Sowa Rigpa name; jatamansi is a major Himalayan plateau drug in the _rGyud bzhi_ register. Some sources also use *ནར་དོ་ (nar do)*, a transliteration of the Sanskrit/Indo-European root.

*Hebrew (biblical):* *נֵרְדְּ (nēred)* — Song of Songs 1:12 ("While the king sitteth at his table, my spikenard sendeth forth the smell thereof") and 4:13–14 ("spikenard and saffron, calamus and cinnamon... myrrh and aloes, with all the chief spices") — the biblical _nēred_ is identified by the standard philological consensus with _Nardostachys jatamansi_, imported into Iron-Age Palestine via the Persian Gulf and Arabian trade. In the New Testament, the πιστικῆς νάρδου (_pistikēs nárdou_) of Mark 14:3 (Mary of Bethany anointing Jesus's feet) and John 12:3 — explicitly described as costing 300 denarii (roughly a year's labor wages) — confirms the herb's status as one of the most precious of all ancient luxury aromatics.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*甘松 (gānsōng)* is classical in the Chinese tradition, with treatment in the _Tang Bencao_ (659) and the _Bencao Gangmu_. The Chinese clinical register is *Spleen-Stomach qi-moving and constraint-opening* — less developed than the Indian _medhya_ (nervine / intellect-supporting) register, but classically distinctive.#footnote[甘松 entries: _Tang Bencao_ (新修本草, 659); _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Warm (溫); some sources mark slightly warm to neutral. (_Cross-system note:_ the Tibb mizāj for jatamansi is *Hot 1°–2°, Dry 2°*; the Ayurvedic vīrya is *śīta* (cooling) — a *real cross-system divergence on the warming/cooling axis*. The Chinese reading aligns with the Tibb; the Indian reading goes against both. This is one of the more interesting classical disagreements in the materia medica and reflects the herb's use-context: Indian use is heavily for *Pitta-pacification* and sleep (cooling register), Chinese and Tibb use leans on *qi-stagnation* and digestive warming.)

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān), Pungent (辛 xīn) — the warming-aromatic-fragrant profile.

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃); some sources add Heart (心).

*Actions & indications:*
- *理氣止痛 (lǐ qì zhǐ tòng)* — regulates qi and stops pain; the cardinal action — for qi-stagnation epigastric pain, abdominal pain, distention.
- *開鬱醒脾 (kāi yù xǐng pí)* — opens constraint, rouses the Spleen; for the depressive-stagnation pattern with poor appetite, sluggish digestion, sighing, mild emotional constraint.
- *外用 — 殺蟲止癢 (topical use — kills parasites, stops itch)* — for tinea-class fungal skin conditions; external use only.
- *Aromatic-bath use* — 甘松 baths are a traditional Chinese aromatic-bath ingredient for fatigue and depressive-stagnation.

*Cautions:* yin-vacuity heat; qi-vacuity without stagnation; pregnancy at high therapeutic doses (general caution for strong-aromatic herbs). 甘松 is not a major drug in modern Chinese clinical practice — appears as an aromatic adjunct rather than a primary herb in most formulas.#footnote[Actions and cautions: _Bencao Gangmu_ 甘松 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; modern Chinese pharmacology texts (_Zhong Yao Xue_, People's Medical Publishing House). The clinical center of gravity for jatamansi is the Indian register, not the Chinese.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

*Jaṭāmāṃsī* is one of the *major medhya (intellect-and-memory-promoting) herbs* of classical Ayurveda and *the principal Indian nervine sedative*. Treated extensively by Charaka, Suśruta, Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, and Bhāvaprakāśa; classed in the _karpūrādi-varga_ (the cooling-aromatic group) of Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu. Used as a primary medhya in the _medhya rasāyana_ register and in numerous sleep-promoting and mental-disorder formulations.#footnote[Jaṭāmāṃsī in Charaka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 4 (_ṣaḍvirecanaśatāśritīya_, the 600-emetic-and-purgative-drugs chapter) — Charaka classes jaṭāmāṃsī among major drugs; Sūtrasthāna 27; Cikitsāsthāna 1 (the _rasāyana_ chapter) — as a *medhya*; Suśruta Saṃhitā; Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, Uttarasthāna 6 (the _bhūta-vidyā_ / mental-disorder section). Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Karpūrādi-varga_; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, _jaṭāmāṃsī_ entry.]

*Rasa (taste):* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent), Madhura (मधुर, sweet — mild).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous) — the slightly snigdha quality is unusual for a bitter-astringent and reflects the rhizome's resinous-oily nature.

*Vīrya (potency):* *Śīta (शीत, cooling)* — *the defining Ayurvedic feature*. Despite the herb's warming-aromatic profile in the Tibb and Chinese readings, the Indian classical assignment is firmly cooling — and this anchors the herb's distinctive *Pitta-pacifying nervine* role: jaṭāmāṃsī is the cooling nervine, contrasting with the warming-nervine herbs of the Indian pharmacy (e.g. _vaca_, _kuṣṭha_).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of jaṭāmāṃsī: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Karpūrādi-varga_; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II. The śīta vīrya despite the warming-aromatic character is the canonical Indian-system distinguishing feature; it places jaṭāmāṃsī among the cooling nervines, distinct from the warming nervines (vaca, kuṣṭha) and aligning it with sandalwood, khus, and the karpūrādi-varga generally.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* *Medhya — intellect-and-memory-promoting; nidrājanana — sleep-promoting; manas-prasādana — mind-quieting / clarifying.* These are *prabhāva-level assignments* in the classical Indian tradition — actions *not directly predictable* from rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka alone (a bitter-astringent-cooling herb might equally well *not* be sedative, hypnotic, or nootropic; the empirical observation of these specific actions on the mind elevates the action category to prabhāva). The *medhya* and *nidrājanana* prabhāvas are *classical*, treated by Charaka and Bhāvaprakāśa explicitly. This contrasts cleanly with the Chinese reading (no comparable cognitive register) and converges with the Greco-Roman / Tibb register (where the herb is recognized as a cardiotonic, mood-elevating, and dream-promoting aromatic).#footnote[The classical medhya prabhāva of jaṭāmāṃsī: Charaka, Cikitsāsthāna 1 (_rasāyana_ chapter); the medhya-rasāyana drugs include _maṇḍūkaparṇī_ (Centella), _yaṣṭimadhu_ (licorice), _guḍūcī_ (Tinospora), _śaṅkhapuṣpī_ (Convolvulus / Evolvulus), and _jaṭāmāṃsī_. The prabhāva-level assignment is canonical and not a modern interpretive elevation.]

*Doṣa effect:* Tridoṣa-pacifying (tridoṣa-śāmaka) — pacifies *all three doṣas*, an unusual classical attribution: pacifies Vāta (the snigdha-laghu-aromatic and nervine register), Pitta (the cooling vīrya and bitter-cooling profile), and Kapha (the bitter-astringent-aromatic register). This rare tridoṣa-pacifying status — shared with very few other classical dravyas (fennel, sandalwood, vetiver, gokṣura) — underlies jaṭāmāṃsī's wide applicability and its place in many compound preparations across diverse clinical contexts.

*Karma (actions):*
- _Medhya_ — intellect-and-memory-promoting (the prabhāva).
- _Nidrājanana_ — sleep-promoting (the prabhāva); the classical Indian hypnotic-nervine.
- _Manas-prasādana_ — mind-quieting / clarifying.
- _Smṛti-vardhana_ — memory-enhancing.
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic.
- _Dāhahara_ — relieves burning sensation (the Pitta-pacifying register).
- _Mūtrala_ — diuretic (mild).
- _Kṛmighna_ — anthelmintic (external use against skin parasites).
- _Tvacya_ — skin-benefiting (used in many cosmetic and dermatological preparations).
- _Used in:_ _unmāda_ (insanity / mood disorders), _apasmāra_ (epilepsy), _anidrā_ (insomnia), _bhrama_ (vertigo), _hṛdroga_ (heart disease), _viṣama-jvara_ (intermittent fever), _kuṣṭha_ (skin disorders — external use), _grahaṇī_ (sprue / chronic digestive disorders), _atisāra_ (diarrhea, mild), _kṣaya_ (wasting). The *unmāda / apasmāra / anidrā / bhrama / smṛti-bhraṃśa* (mood, epilepsy, insomnia, vertigo, memory-loss) cluster is the classical clinical center of gravity — the herb is the principal Indian *neuropsychiatric* drug.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S) Conditional viruddha:* The tridoṣa-pacifying profile makes generic conditional contraindications relatively few. Acute fever with chills (when warming would be preferred); deep cold-pattern digestive states (cold-stomach pattern, where warming herbs would be more appropriate); pregnancy at high therapeutic doses (general caution for strong aromatic-nervine herbs).
- *(N/F) Combination cautions:* in the medhya-rasāyana register, the proper preparation (with milk, ghee, honey, sugar) matters considerably — dry powder taken without snigdha vehicle is folk-cautioned for chronic use.
- *(M) Modern medical:*
  - *Sedative interaction:* the herb has documented sedative-hypnotic activity; combine cautiously with benzodiazepines, barbiturates, opioids, and other CNS depressants; potentiation likely.
  - *Antihypertensive interaction:* modest blood-pressure-lowering activity; monitor with antihypertensive medications.
  - *Pregnancy:* high-dose therapeutic use should be avoided; small culinary / aromatic use is generally safe.
  - *Hepatic concerns:* preclinical data suggest hepatoprotective effects; no specific hepatotoxicity reported, but as with any chronically-used herb, monitoring is sensible.
  - *Allergy / contact dermatitis:* the essential oil can cause contact reactions in sensitive individuals.
  - *Sourcing concern: CITES Appendix II.* Wild _Nardostachys jatamansi_ is listed as endangered under CITES Appendix II due to over-collection for the Indian, Tibb, and Western herbal markets — *ethical sourcing requires verified sustainable cultivation, not wild-harvested* material. This is the single most important modern concern for the herb. Use of cultivated stock or carefully-sourced supplies is required for ethical practice.#footnote[Conditional cautions and modern medical: standard pharmacology references on jaṭāmāṃsī; CITES Appendix II listing for _Nardostachys jatamansi_, verified on the current CITES species database. The sourcing issue is the most pressing modern concern for jatamansi and should be flagged prominently in any clinical or supply context.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Sunbul al-ṭīb_ is *classical* in Tibb, treated by Avicenna in _al-Qānūn_ Book II, Ibn al-Bayṭār in _al-Jāmiʿ_, and across the Unani compendia. The herb is one of the great *aromatic cardiotonic-and-cerebral-tonic* drugs of the Tibb register and a canonical component of *mufarriḥ* (mood-elevating) compound preparations and *Khamīrah* (cardiotonic electuary) class formulas. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the framework.

*Arabic:* سُنبُل الطِّيب (_sunbul al-ṭīb_) — "the fragrant spike"; also سُنبُل هِندي (_sunbul hindī_), "Indian spike" — both interchangeable in classical Unani. Persian: سُنبُل الطیب (_sonbol al-ṭīb_). Urdu: سُنبُل الطیب; جٹاماسی (_jaṭāmāsī_, Sanskrit borrowing).

*Mizāj:* *Hot in the 1st–2nd degree, Dry in the 2nd degree* per Avicenna (_al-Qānūn_ II, _sunbul_). The Tibb classification is *warming*, contrasting with the Indian _śīta vīrya_ — the cross-system divergence noted above. The Tibb reading aligns with the herb's *cardiotonic* and *mufarriḥ* use (which is heating-and-elevating in the Tibb register), while the Indian reading aligns with its *nidrājanana* / sleep-promoting use (which is cooling-and-settling in the Ayurvedic register). Both readings are internally coherent; the divergence reflects different clinical-use emphases as much as a substantive disagreement.

*Afʿāl:*
- *Muqawwī al-qalb* — *cardiotonic* — the cardinal Tibb action; for weak heart, palpitations, sinking-spirit conditions.
- *Mufarriḥ* — *mood-elevating* — for melancholy, sadness, sinking-spirit; central use.
- *Muqawwī al-dimāgh* — *brain-tonic* — for memory, concentration; the cross-system parallel to the Ayurvedic *medhya*.
- *Mufattiḥ* — deobstruent (hepatic, splenic, uterine).
- *Mudirr li-l-bawl* — diuretic (mild).
- *Mudirr li-l-ḥayḍ* — emmenagogue.
- *Munaqqī li-l-dam* — blood-purifying.
- *Kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* — carminative.
- *Muḍādd al-tashannuj* — antispasmodic.
- *Munawwim* — sleep-promoting; the classical convergence with the Ayurvedic *nidrājanana*.

*Istemālāt:* heart weakness; melancholy; palpitations; insomnia; memory loss; mental fatigue; cold-pattern dyspepsia; amenorrhea; hysteria (the classical Tibb _ihtibās al-ṭamth_ register); fever (modest, often as adjunct); convalescence; aromatic-cosmetic preparation.

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt:* hot-mizāj patients in excess; pregnancy at high therapeutic dose; epilepsy (general caution for strong aromatics — though paradoxically also used for some seizure conditions); the classical Tibb sources note that excessive use of warming aromatics can cause headache and insomnia.

*Muṣliḥ:* rose-water (_māʾ al-ward_); sugar / sharbat; sandalwood (_ṣandal_) — to balance the heating-aromatic intensity.

*Badal:* *Saʿd kūfī* (cyperus rotundus / nutgrass — a closely-paralleled aromatic-cardiotonic in Tibb); *ʿūd* (agarwood); *zaʿfarān* (saffron) — for the cardiotonic-mufarriḥ register.

*Classical Tibb formulas:*
- *Khamīrah-i Marwārīd* (خميرة المرواريد, "Pearl Khamīrah") — the great Unani cardiotonic-cerebral-tonic compound electuary, with sunbul al-ṭīb as one of the major aromatic ingredients alongside pearl, saffron, sandalwood, and rose. Used for heart weakness, palpitations, melancholic conditions, and convalescence. One of the most prestigious of all Unani _murakkabāt_.
- *Khamīrah-i Gāo Zaban ʿAnbarī* — bugloss-and-ambergris cardiotonic; sunbul as supporting aromatic.
- *Maʿjūn-i Mufarriḥ* — mood-elevating electuary; sunbul as canonical ingredient.
- *Itrīfal* preparations of various types — sunbul as supporting aromatic / cardiotonic in compound formulas.
- *Sufūf-i Sunbul* — sunbul powder, used in compound prescriptions for sleep, anxiety, and palpitations.
- *Bath / aromatic preparations* — sunbul al-ṭīb in compound aromatic baths for cardiotonic and mufarriḥ effect, used in Persian and Mughal court medicine.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *No direct Qurʾānic or canonical hadith provenance.* The herb is not mentioned in the Qurʾān or in the major hadith collections. The closest classical-Islamic register is its use in the Madinan court of the Prophet's successors and the later caliphates as a luxury aromatic-medicinal (verified through historical sources, not through hadith); the herb's status in Tibb is *Avicennan / Galenic-Arabic*, not Prophetic-medicine-attested. The biblical-Christian "spikenard" of Mark 14:3 (Mary of Bethany anointing Jesus) does not transfer into the Islamic-attestation register, but is worth noting as a *cross-tradition recognition of the herb's preciousness* in the late-classical Levant.#footnote[Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II, _sunbul_ entries — substantial treatment; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, _sunbul_ and _sunbul hindī_ entries; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_, vol. III, _sunbul al-ṭīb_ entry; Hakim Mohammad Kabīruddīn, _Bayāz-e Kabīr_. _Khamīrah-i Marwārīd_ formula: standard Unani formulary; AYUSH _National Formulary of Unani Medicine_. The cross-system Pitta-pacifying / Tibb-warming divergence is a substantive classical disagreement reflecting different clinical-use emphases.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *甘松 in aromatic compound formulas* — generally as a supporting aromatic for opening-constraint and digestive use; not a primary herb in any major canonical formula.
- *External aromatic baths* with 甘松 + other warming aromatics — for fatigue and depressive-stagnation.
- *Topical paste preparations* — for fungal skin conditions.#footnote[Chinese formula uses: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; the herb is a minor Chinese drug compared to its central status in Ayurveda and Tibb.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Sārasvata cūrṇa, Brāhmī ghṛta, Sārasvatāriṣṭa, Saṃvardhana-ghṛta* — the major *medhya* (intellect-promoting) compound preparations frequently include jaṭāmāṃsī as a canonical ingredient.
- *Mahānārāyaṇa taila, Pinḍa taila* — classical medicated oils for nervous-system, sleep, and pain conditions; jaṭāmāṃsī as canonical aromatic-nervine ingredient.
- *Manasamitra vatakam* — a famous compound preparation for mental conditions; jaṭāmāṃsī is one of the principal ingredients.
- *Jaṭāmāṃsī cūrṇa* — the simple powder, taken with warm milk and ghee, typically 1–3 g at bedtime for insomnia or 0.5–1 g twice daily for memory and anxiety.
- *Jaṭāmāṃsī taila / arka / kṣīrapāka* — oil, distillate, milk decoction — various preparation classes.
- *Cikitsākramavalī, Bhaiṣajya-ratnāvalī* — extensive classical formulary uses; many compound medhya and nidrājanana preparations.
- *Mūrcchanā preparations* — the classical Ayurvedic process of repeatedly cooking ghee or oil with herbal pastes — jaṭāmāṃsī is one of the canonical mūrcchanā herbs.#footnote[Ayurvedic formulary preparations: Sharangadhara Saṃhitā; Bhaiṣajya-ratnāvalī; _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Parts I and II; the AYUSH database. Jaṭāmāṃsī is one of the most-used medhya herbs in classical formulary practice.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman: Dioscorides* in _De Materia Medica_ I.6–7 treats *spikenard* (_νάρδος_) extensively, distinguishing *Indian nard* (νάρδος ἰνδική, the genuine _N. jatamansi_), *Syrian nard*, and *Celtic nard* — the first being the most precious and the genuine spikenard of trade. Used for digestive, diuretic, emmenagogue, sleep-promoting, and as an ingredient in many compound preparations including the famous Roman *nardinum* ointment. *Pliny* in _Naturalis Historia_ XII–XIII gives extensive economic and cultivar data; nardinum was an enormously expensive ointment, valued at *100 denarii per Roman pound* in some sources — comparable to gold by weight. *Galen* treats nardus medicinally.
- *Roman luxury and cosmetic use:* nardus / spikenard was one of the principal ingredients of the *unguenta* (luxury ointments) of the Roman aristocracy, mentioned by Horace, Catullus, and Pliny as the most precious of all anointing aromatics; the Mark 14:3 / John 12:3 narrative of Mary of Bethany anointing Jesus's feet with "spikenard" (νάρδου πιστικῆς, "true spikenard") and the disciples objecting to the cost (300 denarii — roughly a year's wages for a labourer) is precise about the herb's value.
- *Biblical attestation:* the Hebrew _nēred_ of Song of Songs 1:12 and 4:13–14 (in the great catalogue of luxurious aromatics), and the New Testament Greek νάρδος as above. The Christian liturgical and devotional tradition has carried "spikenard" forward continuously as the canonical anointing-aromatic of the Magdalene narrative.
- *Medieval European pharmacy:* spikenard appears in many compound preparations of the Salernitan and later European pharmacy; as a stomachic, cardiotonic, emmenagogue, and sleep-promoting drug. It is a canonical ingredient of the great theriacs.
- *Modern Western herbal:* the dried rhizome is used by modern phytotherapy as an *essential oil* and a tincture for nervous-system support; the modern Western interest is largely a renewed engagement with the Indian (Ayurvedic) and Tibb registers. ESCOP does not have a dedicated monograph.#footnote[Greco-Roman sources: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ I.6–7 — Beck translation (Olms-Weidmann); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XII–XIII (Loeb / Rackham) — extensive nard economy data. The 300-denarii valuation of John 12:3 / Mark 14:3 is roughly a year's wages for a Roman labourer; the herb's status as one of the most precious of all ancient aromatics is undisputed. Theriac inclusion: Galenic theriac literature; Salernitan compound preparations.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above. The Tibb formulary use of sunbul al-ṭīb is *extensively elaborated*, especially in the cardiotonic-mufarriḥ register: *Khamīrah-i Marwārīd*, *Khamīrah-i Gāo Zaban ʿAnbarī*, *Maʿjūn-i Mufarriḥ*, *Sufūf-i Sunbul*, the great compound electuaries.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_Nardostachys jatamansi_ is *uniquely climatically narrow* in its native range: the *high Himalayan alpine zone, 3000–5000 m elevation* — Nepal, Sikkim, Bhutan, the north Indian Himalayan states (Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh), and the Tibetan plateau fringe. This is the *cold-dry high-altitude* climate par excellence — Sowa Rigpa's home climate — and the herb's *climate of growth* is among the most extreme on the planet for a non-cushion herb. The herb has, however, *traveled across all the great Old-World climate zones as a luxury aromatic* — to hot-arid Levant and Mediterranean, to hot-humid South India, to cool-damp Atlantic Europe (via the Roman empire and the Christian church), and to the continental Chinese plain (via the Silk Road) — making it a striking case of a *single-native-climate herb with pan-climatic use across the ancient world*. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Cold-dry high-altitude (Himalayan alpine, the native climate)

The Sowa Rigpa home climate. Extreme Vāta climate; cold-dry-windy; 寒 + 燥 + 風; Kidney-yang and Lung loaded. The herb grows in *meadow / scrub at the edge of the snow line* — its existence is itself a Himalayan-alpine survival adaptation, with the rhizome packed with resin and essential oil to survive the freeze-thaw cycle.

*Traditional preparation in the native climate.* The local Himalayan and Tibetan plateau communities use jatamansi in *aromatic-warming compound preparations*: it is one of the canonical aromatic ingredients of the *Tibetan incense* tradition (along with juniper, sandalwood, agarwood), used in temples and monasteries; the Tibetan medical _rGyud bzhi_ includes it in compound nervine and cardiotonic preparations; the high-altitude folk use is for *digestive cold-pattern* and as a *winter aromatic*. The local use is *warming* — aligning with the Tibb / Chinese reading — reflecting the climatic need.

*Cares.* Traditional sustainable harvesting practices preserved by Tibetan and Nepali highland communities historically maintained populations; the *modern commercial collection for the Indian, Tibb, and Western herbal market is now the principal driver of wild-population collapse* — the herb is *CITES Appendix II*. Ethical sourcing requires verified cultivation, not wild-harvest.

=== Hot-humid South India (Ayurveda) — luxury aromatic and nervine import

In South India's hot-humid monsoonal climate, jatamansi enters as a *Himalayan import* via north Indian and Tibetan trade. The herb is used in the *cooling Ayurvedic register* — the śīta vīrya reading aligns with the climatic need in hot-humid Pitta-Kapha conditions: *medhya rasāyana* preparations with jatamansi + brāhmī + maṇḍūkaparṇī + śaṅkhapuṣpī are cool-snigdha-sweet brain-and-mind tonics for the Pitta-loaded southern Indian summer; *jaṭāmāṃsī taila* and *medhya ghṛta* are taken as cooling-snigdha mūrcchana preparations.

*Cares.* The Indian Pitta-pacifying use is climatically apt for the hot-humid South — the cooling vīrya counters the hot-humid climate's Pitta loading; the snigdha vehicle counters the dryness of the alpine rhizome.

=== Hot-arid Levant / Mediterranean (Tibb and Greco-Roman) — luxury aromatic import

Spikenard entered the Mediterranean and Levantine world via the *Persian Gulf and Arabian trade*, transported overland through the spice routes from Tibet → north India → Persia → Babylon → the Levantine ports → Rome. By the Roman imperial period, _nardus indica_ was *one of the most precious of all aromatic substances* (the Mary of Bethany anointing). The Tibb register continued and codified Greco-Roman use, with substantial elaboration in the Avicennan cardiotonic-mufarriḥ framework.

*Traditional preparation.* In the hot-arid Levant and Mediterranean, spikenard appears as the *expensive aromatic ingredient* in *nardinum* (Roman ointment), *Khamīrah-i Marwārīd* (Unani electuary), Sharbat preparations, and luxury-cosmetic combinations. The use is *cardiotonic and mufarriḥ* — fitting hot-arid Pitta-Vāta constitutional needs for *anti-melancholic* and *cardiotonic* register.

*Cares.* In hot-arid climates, jatamansi was used in *small medicinal doses in compound preparations* with cooling adjuvants (rose-water, sandalwood); the rich Tibb _muṣliḥ_ pairing of sunbul with cooling sandalwood is precisely this climatic-corrective synthesis. The herb is not a daily dietary item but a *clinical and aromatic-luxury* drug.

=== Continental Chinese plain (Tang-era introduction) — luxury aromatic and digestive drug

Introduced via the Tang-dynasty Silk Road from the Himalayan range; the Chinese clinical use is *qi-stagnation digestive* and aromatic, climatically apt for the continental seasonal-extreme zone's swing patterns.

*Traditional preparation.* 甘松 in compound aromatic formulas and aromatic baths; used as a luxury aromatic in some imperial preparations.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The modern Western yoga / mindfulness / aromatherapy movement has globalized jaṭāmāṃsī essential oil for diffusion, massage, and "calming" applications across all climates and seasons — typically without reference to the classical mizāj or vīrya frameworks, and without distinction between climates and constitutional types. The classical readings — *Ayurvedic cooling-snigdha-medhya for Pitta-loaded conditions and Indian summer; Tibb warming-cardiotonic for cold-pattern and melancholic conditions; Chinese qi-moving for stagnation* — give finer guidance than the flattened modern register. *The most pressing modern transposition concern, however, is not climate-mismatch but sustainability:* wild-harvested jaṭāmāṃsī for the modern aromatherapy market is the principal driver of the herb's CITES-listed endangered status. The *ethical use* concern dwarfs the *climatic* concern in modern practice. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework; see `climates.typ` for the cold-dry high-altitude profile.#footnote[Climate framework: see `climates.typ`. The Silk-Road luxury aromatic trade in spikenard: standard historical literature on the ancient spice trade (e.g. Miller, _The Spice Trade of the Roman Empire_; Sidebotham, _Berenike and the Ancient Maritime Spice Route_). CITES Appendix II status: the principal modern sustainability and ethical-sourcing concern.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

The herb is not a dietary item — it is a *therapeutic medicinal*; "nutrition" data are not relevant. Composition is given for the dried rhizome and its essential oil.

*Key constituents (dried rhizome):*
- *Essential oil (0.5–2.5% of dried rhizome):* the principal active fraction; dominated by sesquiterpenes and sesquiterpene esters. Major compounds: *jatamansone (valeranone)*, *spirojatamol*, *nardostachone*, *nardol*, *β-eudesmol*, *valencene*, *patchouli alcohol*, *aristolene*. The compound profile is similar to but distinct from *valerian* (the closest pharmacological cousin); the sesquiterpene-rich oil is the basis of both the aroma and the sedative-nervine activity.
- *Sesquiterpene coumarins and lignans.*
- *Phenolic compounds:* including chlorogenic acid, ursolic acid derivatives.
- *Polysaccharides* in the root: modest immunomodulatory potential.
- *Alkaloids (modest):* actinidine and related; trace.
- *Rhizome resin:* the dense, dark resin contains the principal aroma compounds and pharmacological actives.#footnote[Phytochemistry of jatamansi: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); reviews in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytochemistry_, _Pharmacognosy Reviews_; the sesquiterpene profile is the principal active fraction.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Sedative-hypnotic and anxiolytic* — preclinical and modest clinical evidence; mechanism via GABA-system modulation (similar to valerian). The classical _nidrājanana_ prabhāva has clean phytopharmacological support.
- *Nootropic / memory-enhancing* — preclinical evidence (acetylcholinesterase inhibition, neuroprotection, antioxidant activity in CNS tissue); modest clinical evidence; supports the classical _medhya_ prabhāva.
- *Antidepressant* — preclinical evidence in animal depression models; modest clinical evidence; the classical Tibb _mufarriḥ_ register has phytopharmacological support.
- *Anticonvulsant* — preclinical evidence; preliminary clinical; supports the classical use in _apasmāra_ (epilepsy).
- *Cardiovascular* — modest blood-pressure lowering; cardiotonic activity in preclinical models; supports the classical Tibb _muqawwī al-qalb_ register.
- *Hepatoprotective* — preclinical evidence in chemical liver-injury models; modest clinical evidence.
- *Antimicrobial* — broad-spectrum in vitro activity from the essential oil.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — preclinical evidence; modest clinical.
- *Neuroprotective* — preclinical evidence in models of Alzheimer's, Parkinson's, ischemic stroke; promising but preliminary.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Powder (cūrṇa):* 1–3 g per day, typically at bedtime, with warm milk and ghee for medhya use; 0.5–1 g twice daily for memory and anxiety.
- *Decoction:* 2–5 g of dried rhizome per cup; once or twice daily.
- *Tincture:* 1–3 mL of 1:5 tincture, two or three times daily (modern Western register).
- *Essential oil (Western aromatherapy):* topical use only, dilute heavily; not for ingestion in typical aromatherapy practice.
- *Chinese 甘松 clinical dose:* 3–6 g in decoction.
- *Unani Sunbul al-ṭīb:* 1–3 g in compound preparations.
- *Pregnancy:* avoid therapeutic doses (uterine-stimulant and CNS-active); small culinary / aromatic use generally safe; verify against current obstetric guidance.
- *Concurrent medication:* sedatives and CNS depressants (potentiation); antihypertensives (modest additive effect); monitor.
- *Sustainability — the principal modern concern:* *use only cultivated or verified-sustainable-source material*. Wild-collection has driven the herb to CITES Appendix II status; ethical practice requires cultivated supply. The Ministry of Ayurveda (AYUSH) in India has supported cultivation projects in Uttarakhand and Himachal Pradesh; verify the source provenance with the supplier.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (for 甘松); P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II (for jaṭāmāṃsī); AYUSH _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_; Unani _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_; modern phytotherapy literature. CITES Appendix II: verify on current CITES species database; sustainable-cultivation projects in Himalayan states are the appropriate sourcing context.]

